---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, COMPANY, META_PIXEL_ID } from "../lib/site";

/**
 * Written against what the code actually does, not a generic template:
 * every processor and field named below is one the site really touches.
 * When a data flow changes, this page changes with it — in particular
 * src/pages/api/lead.ts, src/lib/db.ts, src/lib/newsletter-store.mjs,
 * src/lib/mailerlite.mjs, src/lib/meta-capi.ts and src/layouts/Base.astro.
 *
 * noindex, like /thanks/ and the newsletter utility pages: this has to exist
 * and be linked, not rank. `noindex, follow` still lets crawlers read it.
 */

const updated = "22 September 2026";
---

<Base
  title={`Privacy Policy — ${SITE.name}`}
  description="What this site collects, why, who processes it, how long it is kept, and the rights you have over it."
  noindex
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <span class="kicker">Legal</span>
      <h1 class="page-head__title">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede">
        What this site collects, why it collects it, who else sees it, and what you can ask us
        to do about it.
      </p>
      <p class="post__date">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose--narrow">
      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        This site is operated by <strong>{COMPANY.legalName}</strong>, a{" "}
        {COMPANY.jurisdiction} {COMPANY.entityType} trading as {SITE.name} at kastriottanaj.com.
        The company is the data controller for everything described here. {SITE.name} runs it
        from {SITE.locality}, {SITE.countryName}, and the servers are in Germany.
      </p>
      <p>
        Email: <a href="mailto:kastriot@kastriottanaj.com">kastriot@kastriottanaj.com</a><br />
        Phone: <a href={`tel:${SITE.phone}`}>{SITE.phoneDisplay}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <mark
          >REVIEW: add a postal address you actually receive mail at. Do NOT use the registered
          agent's address ({COMPANY.registeredOffice}) — that is a service-of-process address
          shared by thousands of companies, and a data subject writing there reaches a mail
          handler, not you. Article 13 wants a contact point that works.</mark
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <mark
          >REVIEW: the controller is now a United States company, so it sits outside the EU/EEA
          and Article 27 still applies — forming in Delaware sharpened this question rather than
          settling it. An EU representative is required unless the Article 27(2) exemption fits:
          processing that is occasional, excludes large-scale special-category data, and is
          unlikely to risk people's rights. A lead form and a newsletter may well fit, but that is
          a lawyer's call, not a guess. Name the representative here if one is appointed.</mark
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <mark
          >REVIEW: do not publish until the filed certificate is back from Delaware. Naming an
          entity that does not legally exist yet is worse than naming none.</mark
        >
      </p>

      <h2>The contact form</h2>
      <p>
        When you send the form on the contact page we receive your name, email address, the
        service you selected and your message. Along with it the server records your IP address,
        your browser's user-agent string and the page that referred you.
      </p>
      <p>
        That record is written to a database on our own server and emailed to us. The email is a
        notification; the database is the copy that lasts, so a bounce or a spam folder is never
        the difference between you writing to us and us never knowing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Why:</strong> to answer you and to take steps you asked for before a possible
        contract. The IP, user-agent and referrer are kept for security and abuse prevention,
        which is our legitimate interest in keeping the form usable.
      </p>

      <h2>The newsletter</h2>
      <p>
        Subscribing stores your email address, the page you subscribed from, your IP address,
        your user-agent and the relevant timestamps. Nothing is ever sent to an address that has
        not confirmed itself: you receive a confirmation link first, and the subscription only
        becomes real when you click it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Confirmed subscribers are also passed to <strong>MailerLite</strong>, which delivers the
        emails on our behalf. Every email carries an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing takes
        effect immediately.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Why:</strong> your consent, which you can withdraw at any time.</p>

      <h2>Analytics and advertising cookies</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing optional runs until you choose. The banner you saw on your first visit sets
        Google Consent Mode to denied by default, and the Meta Pixel's script is not even
        downloaded until you accept — so rejecting is not a promise, it is the absence of a
        request.
      </p>
      <p>If you accept, two things are switched on:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> — how people find and move through the site, in
          aggregate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Meta Pixel</strong> (ID {META_PIXEL_ID}) — measuring and personalising
          advertising.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        With advertising consent given, a lead you submit is also sent to Meta's Conversions API
        from our server. Your email address and name are hashed before they leave the server, so
        Meta receives an irreversible fingerprint rather than the values themselves. Your IP
        address and user-agent are sent as they are, together with Meta's own <code>_fbp</code>
        and <code>_fbc</code> cookies where they exist. Without advertising consent, none of this
        is sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Why:</strong> your consent. You can change it at any time with the
        <strong>Cookie settings</strong> link in the footer of every page.
      </p>

      <h2>Spam protection and server logs</h2>
      <p>
        Every form carries a hidden field that real people never fill in, and submissions are
        rate-limited per IP address. Cloudflare Turnstile may also be used to tell people from
        bots; where it is, Cloudflare receives your IP address and a token for that check alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        The web server keeps standard access logs — IP address, timestamp, the URL requested, the
        referrer and the user-agent — which roll over automatically.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Why:</strong> our legitimate interest in keeping the site up and free of abuse.</p>

      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Fonts are served from this domain. Visiting a page sends no request to Google Fonts and
          discloses nothing to them.
        </li>
        <li>We do not sell personal data, and we do not buy it.</li>
        <li>There is no automated decision-making and no profiling that produces legal effects.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who else processes your data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hetzner</strong> — the server the site and its database run on (Germany).</li>
        <li><strong>Hostinger</strong> — outgoing email for form notifications and confirmations.</li>
        <li><strong>MailerLite</strong> — newsletter delivery.</li>
        <li><strong>Google</strong> — Analytics, only with consent.</li>
        <li><strong>Meta</strong> — Pixel and Conversions API, only with consent.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — Turnstile bot checks, where enabled.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Some of these are outside the EU/EEA. Where that is the case, transfers rely on the
        European Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses or an adequacy decision.
        <mark
          >REVIEW: confirm the transfer mechanism each provider actually offers you, and name it
          here.</mark
        >
      </p>

      <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Contact form enquiries</strong> — <mark>REVIEW: confirm a period, e.g. 24
          months</mark> after our last contact, then deleted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Newsletter subscribers</strong> — until you unsubscribe. Unconfirmed
          subscriptions are discarded rather than kept.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Server access logs</strong> — rolled over on a short cycle, not archived.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Analytics and advertising data</strong> — held by Google and Meta under their own
          retention settings.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you can ask us to give you a copy of your data, correct it, delete it,
        restrict or object to how it is used, or hand it to another provider in a portable form.
        Where processing rests on consent, you can withdraw that consent at any time — for cookies
        through the footer link, for the newsletter through any unsubscribe link — and withdrawing
        it does not affect what was lawful beforehand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:kastriot@kastriottanaj.com">kastriot@kastriottanaj.com</a> and we will
        answer within one month.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you think we have handled your data badly you can complain to a data protection
        supervisory authority — in the EU, the one where you live or work.
        <mark
          >REVIEW: with a US controller and no EU establishment there is no single lead authority;
          complaints go to the authority where the complainant lives, or to the one in the member
          state where an Article 27 representative is based. Settle the representative question
          above first, then word this to match.</mark
        >
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. Material changes to anything
        resting on your consent will be asked for again rather than assumed.
      </p>

      <p>
        See also the <a href="/terms/">Terms &amp; Conditions</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
